      script: `Fourth floor: Next Level. The Concentration Room insists: every text must reveal Christ. And in Philippians 2, Christ doesn't just appear — He is every verb. He existed in the form of God. He did not grasp. He emptied Himself. He took the form of a servant. He was made in the likeness of men. He humbled Himself. He became obedient. Seven verbs, seven deliberate choices, seven steps down the staircase. This is not passive theology — it is the most active, voluntary, deliberate self-offering in cosmic history. No one took His life — He laid it down. No one forced His descent — He chose it. And here's why this matters for your struggle with the Bible: if you've been reading Scripture looking for rules, advice, or moral lessons — you've been looking at the wrong level. The Concentration Room says: look for Christ first. He is the subject of every passage, the hero of every story, the answer to every question. When you put Christ at the center, everything else orbits properly. Without this room, Philippians 2 becomes a theological curiosity or a guilt trip about humility. With it, it becomes a portrait of the most beautiful act in the history of the universe — and an invitation to let that beauty reshape you. The power of Concentration is clarity: when Christ is the center, every other detail finds its proper orbit.`,
