      script: `The Dimensions Room is one of the most powerful rooms in the palace, so let me explain exactly how it works before we apply it. Every passage in the Bible can be read through five distinct lenses — five dimensions — and most people only ever engage one, maybe two. That means they're missing up to eighty percent of what the text is actually saying. Here are the five dimensions. Dimension one is Literal — what does the text plainly say in its original historical and grammatical context? What did it mean to the original audience? Dimension two is Christ — how does this passage reveal, point to, or center on Jesus? This is the non-negotiable lens: every text must be brought to Christ. Dimension three is Personal — how does this text apply to my life right now, today, in my specific situation? Dimension four is Church — what does this text mean for the body of believers as a community across time? And dimension five is Heavenly — how does this text connect to heaven's reality and God's eternal plan? When you learn to read every passage through all five lenses simultaneously, the Bible becomes five times richer. And this is transformative for anyone who reads a passage and thinks, "I got the point — He was humble. What else is there?" There are four more dimensions you haven't seen yet. Now let me show you all five applied to Philippians 2. Literal: Paul, writing from a Roman prison, describes Christ's pre-existence, incarnation, and crucifixion in the most compressed Christological statement in the entire New Testament. This is historical theology at its densest. Christ: the Son of God voluntarily descends from the throne of the universe to the cross of Calvary — from the form of God to the form of a slave — in the most radical act of love ever enacted in cosmic history. Personal — and this is where it gets uncomfortable: "Let this mind be in you" is a daily command. When I want credit, He emptied Himself. When I demand my rights, He didn't grasp. When I refuse a menial task, He washed feet. The gap between His mind and mine is where discipleship happens. Church: the body of Christ should be the most counter-cultural community on earth — not marked by power, prestige, or position, but by mutual servanthood, each esteeming others better than themselves. If your church looks like a corporate ladder, it doesn't look like Philippians 2. Heavenly: verses nine through eleven reveal the exaltation — "every knee shall bow, of things in heaven, and things in earth, and things under the earth." This is the third heaven reality: Christ's kenosis ends in universal sovereignty. Descent leads to coronation. One passage, five dimensions, five distinct revelations. Once you learn to look through all five lenses, the Bible becomes a completely different book.`,
