      title: "Genre Shapes Meaning",
      script: `Connect 6 identifies the genre — and genre awareness is essential because genre determines how you interpret. The Bible contains six major genres, each with its own rules of language. Let me name all six so you have the complete framework. Prophecy uses symbols, codes, timelines, and apocalyptic imagery. Poetry uses metaphor, parallelism, rhythm, and emotional texture. History tells events with theological undercurrents woven beneath the surface. Gospels are narrative centered on Christ's life, teaching, death, and resurrection. Epistles are logical argument and doctrinal instruction, written to specific communities with specific problems. And Parables are simple stories concealing profound depth, designed to reveal truth to the seeking heart. When you misidentify the genre, you misread the text — like reading a love poem as a legal document or reading apocalyptic prophecy as a newspaper. Connect 6 protects you from that error by training you to always identify genre before interpreting content. Now let's apply it to Philippians 2. This passage is widely recognized as an early Christian hymn embedded within a pastoral epistle. That dual genre matters enormously. As a hymn, it has rhythm, parallelism, and theological density — it was meant to be sung, memorized, and repeated in worship. The descent staircase has a poetic cadence: form of God, form of servant; likeness of men, fashion as a man; obedient unto death, even the death of the cross. Each phrase steps lower in both meaning and meter. As an epistle, it carries apostolic authority — Paul isn't suggesting this theology; he's commanding its adoption. "Let this mind be in you" is an imperative from an apostle under Roman chains. The power of genre awareness is this: you read hymns with reverence, because they were crafted for worship, and you read epistles with urgency, because they carry the weight of divine commission. This passage demands both — worship and obedience simultaneously. Genre is the key that unlocks the right door.`,
