      script: `The Fruit Room asks the essential quality-control question: does this study produce the fruit of the Spirit? And this is your personal guardrail against bad interpretation — even your own. Philippians 2 should produce humility — not self-deprecation or false modesty, but Christ-patterned selflessness that genuinely esteems others as more important than yourself. It should produce courage — the willingness to descend when the entire world says ascend, to serve when culture demands you be served, to empty when instinct says accumulate. It should produce joy — and this is the surprising one. Paul frames this hymn in the most joyful letter he ever wrote. Joy and humility are not in tension — they are twins. The person who stops grasping is the person who starts experiencing freedom. Maybe that's the freedom you've been looking for. And it should produce love — because kenosis is love in its purest form: self-giving without guarantee of return. If your study of Philippians 2 produces self-righteousness — "I'm more humble than you" — the interpretation has failed spectacularly. If it produces despair — "I can never be like Christ" — it has missed the word "let." "Let this mind be in you." It's received, not manufactured. You don't have to generate Christ's mind by willpower. You have to open the door and let it in. The Fruit Room catches bad theology by testing its harvest. Good fruit? Keep going. Bad fruit? Go back and check your work.`,
